/** 

@page benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_hpc_assessment HPC assessment

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
@tableofcontents

One difficulty in letting ExaGRyPE run is that there's a plethora of different
setting that one can pick from. This is quite natural for a research code but
quickly becomes challenging for people who just wanna have a go or start
benchmarking. It also means that shipping a proper benchmark is far from 
trivial.

The single black hole benchmark is written in a way such that it supports many
different settings. We can therefore play around with them and find good 
choices. Anecdotal evidence fees into the settings used by @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_production_benchmarks.
All the different benchmarking ingredients for the HPC assessment are available
through the script ```performance-studies.py```. 


# Generic settings

Benchmarking and playing around with the single black hole is never easy, as it is a rather complex setup
already with a rather long initialisation and grid construction phase. However,
it is possible to benchmark it once you disable plots, the tracers, and you use
an unreasonable coarse mesh. That is, we disable a lot of key features for the 
benchmarking runs. In general, we apply the following simplifications:


- We reduce the end time drastically to ```-et 1e-2``` via the Python API front-end.
- We omit any output by defaulting the ```--plot 0``` from the Python call to zero which switches off the plotting.

A typical setup therefore resembles

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 performance-studies.py -et 1e-2 --plot 0.0 ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# Solver, algorithm and optimisation variants

## Solvers

The script allows us to toggle between different solver variants from the command line by 
picking a variant through ```--solver```.

- Finite Volume solvers all start with a ```fv-``` prefix (if you use the 
  command line) followed by the patch size. We exclusively employ enclave
  solvers in this setup, i.e. each Finite Volume solver spawns tasks.


## Load balancing

Proper load balancing between ranks and threads is obviously key for good performance.
The benchmark supports a couple of load balancing schemes to empirically study different concepts:

- ```generic``` uses an instance of toolbox::loadbalancing::strategies::cascade::SpreadOutHierarchically.
  That is, we first populate all ranks and then all threads, unless the Python API
  specifies the value ```trees``` which constraints the maximum number of subpartitions
  (trees) that we employ per rank. 
- ```tailored``` is generic as well, but it uses a cost functional which 
  hard-codes information about the Schwarzschild solution: The closer a cell to
  the centre, the higher its weight.
- ```offline-regular``` uses the offline balancer over a regular mesh, i.e. 
  it uses the cell size to anticipate what a regular mesh would look like and 
  then computes an optimal partitioning before we even start the compute.
  
Constraining the number of trees does not mean that threads idle: We use
task-based parallelism on top of domain decomposition and hence assume that
idle threads grab tasks and hence automatically balance out illpartitioning.




## Tasks scheduling and orchestration

For each benchmarks, there are various schedulers:

- ```--scheduler native``` Picks the native, hardcoded scheduler from Peano. 
  This disables all task fusion and also all GPU offloading.
- ```--scheduler native-piorities``` Constructs an instance of 
  @ref benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::MulticoreOrchestration as task orchestration. It still disables all the 
  task fusion (and therefore GPU offloading), but now activates features 
  such as 
- ```--scheduler gpu-lazy-evaluation``` Tasks are shipped off to the GPU lazily,
  i.e.~when the task results are requested. Compare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler gpu-timed-execution``` Tasks are shipped en bloc to the GPU after
  each mesh sweep that produces tasks. Compare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler gpu-producer-consumer``` Shipped off to the GPU with producer-consumer 
  tasksCompare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler gpu-eager-consumer-tasks``` Tasks are shipped off to the GPU using
  Peano's eager consumer pattern.Compare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler --no-tasks``` No tasking is used, i.e. everything that could be mapped onto a task is executed immediately.


If an orchestration with task fusion is picked and you employ a combined FV-FD 
solver, then only the Finite Volume tasks are sent off to the accelerator. 
Details on this behaviour can be found in 
@ref benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::MulticoreOrchestration.


# Studying the right number of subpartitions per node

We start with some experiments where we artifically restrict the number subpartitions uses on the node.
If we use one subpartition per core, we quickly run into a situation where the individual subpartitions become too small and overhead eats up all scalability improvements.
Therefore, a first step restricts the number of trees through ```--trees```.
While we do so, we first disable all tasking manually, and we also stick exclusively to Finite Volumes:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 --plot 0.0 --scheduler no-tasks --trees ... ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Building all the benchmarks in one go

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm peano-*
rm README-*
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python:../../../../applications/exahype2/ccz4
module load python
for TREES in 1 2 3 4 6 8 9 16 27 32 64 128 256 
do
  python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 -et 0.01       --plot 0.0 --cell-size 0.6 --scheduler no-tasks --trees $TREES --output peano-regular-$TREES-trees
  python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 -et 0.001 -amr --plot 0.0 --cell-size 0.1 --scheduler no-tasks --trees $TREES --output peano-adaptive-$TREES-trees
done
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

There are two job script in the scripts subdirectory, 
which can serve as a starting point if you want to submit experiments to a 
supercomputer with an array job. This script is for @ref page_machines_hamilton "Hamilton 8" but most 
Slurm scripts will look similar.


Once the data is in, it might be best to package it into two archives

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tar -czvf results-adaptive.tar.gz results-adaptive-*
tar -czvf results-regular.tar.gz results-regular-* 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and to copy them over onto a local computer. There is a dedicated script
plot-trees-vs-runtime.py in the scripts subdirectory of the benchmark that can read 
zips and create output data.
The script is based upon the 
@ref page_toolbox_loadbalancing_api_api_postprocessing "load balancing postprocessing scripts"
and yields plots similar to the one below.


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=~/git/Peano/src/toolbox/loadbalancing/api/postprocessing

python3 scripts/plot-trees-vs-runtime.py results-regular.tar.gz   --output trees-vs-runtime-regular
python3 scripts/plot-trees-vs-runtime.py results-adaptive.tar.gz  --output trees-vs-runtime-adaptive
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

## Regular grid outcomes

@image html benchmarks/exahype2/ccz4/single-black-hole/trees-vs-runtime-regular.png

We see that there is a sweet spot whenever we hit the canonical @f$ 3^d @f$ 
subpartition count. We also see that there is limited sense in going beyond
27 subpartitions on a rank. The crucial question here is whether this is a 
general pattern, or if the workload per partition then simply becomes too
small. 

